# data file for fldtk fluid (Fast Light User Interface Designer)
version 1.0000
comment {README FIRST
- - - - - - -
This fluid (.fl) file demonstrates how to create a complete
fldtk program with a button whose callback calls a real (virtual,
by D default) instance method directly.

Unlike FLTK's version of this demo, there is no
static-trampoline-plus-void* method here: FLTK needed that shape
because a C++ callback can't close over `this`. A D closure
created inside a real method body (the constructor, here) already
captures its enclosing instance, so the button's callback can just
call cbBnVirtual(...) directly -- see CONVENTIONS.md's "Callbacks are D
delegates" convention.

Also demonstrates safe self-deletion from inside a widget's own
callback -- see cbBnVirtual's own code comment and
fl.core.deleteWidget()'s doc comment.

Use fluid interactively to open this fluid (.fl) file and
double-click on items to see how they are defined.
}
  {
    in_source
    in_header
  }
class App
  {
    : {Window}
  }
  {
    Function {this(int x, int y, string l)}
      {
        comment {Constructor}
        open
      }
      {
        code {super(x, y, l);
writefln("Creating new App");
auto exitCb = new Button(20, 20, 260, 60, "Exit: invokes virtual callback");
exitCb.callback((o) \{ cbBnVirtual(77); \});
show();}
          {
          }
      }
    Function ~this()
      {
        comment {Virtual destructor}
        open
      }
      {
        code {writefln("Closing and deleting App");}
          {
          }
      }
    Function {cbBnVirtual(int v)}
      {
        comment {Virtual callback method}
        return_type void
        open
      }
      {
        code {writefln("Executing virtual callback cbBnVirtual, value = %d", v);
// NOT destroy(this) -- deleting from inside a widget's own callback
// (this button's callback is still on the call stack) would recursively
// destroy this window's own children (Group.~this() calls clear())
// while one of them is still executing. fl.core.deleteWidget() defers
// the real destroy() to the next wait()/check() iteration instead.
fl.core.deleteWidget(this);}
          {
          }
      }
  }
Function {}
  {
    open
  }
  {
    code {new App(300, 100, "Virtual Callback Test");}
      {
        selected
      }
  }
